<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kitab-i Diyarbakriyya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">15th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kitab-i Diyarbakriyya is a Persian chronicle of the Aq Qoyunlu dynasty written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-bakr-tihrani">Abu Bakr Tihrani</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a> in the later fifteenth century. It recounts the rise of the Bayandur <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> princes from their base around Diyarbakır and supports their legitimacy with a genealogy reaching back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-khan">Oghuz Khan</a>, the legendary ancestor of the Turkmens. It is the most important narrative source for early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> history.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Supreme power belonged to the Bayandur house, whose head took the titles of sultan and padishah and claimed descent from Oghuz Khan, the legendary ancestor of the Turkmens, an ideology celebrated in Abu Bakr Tihrani’s court chronicle, the <b>Kitab-i Diyarbakriyya</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Jahan Shah wrote poetry in Azerbaijani and Persian, and Abu Bakr Tehrani's <b>Kitab-i Diyarbakriyya</b> chronicled the dynasty in Persian.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-qoyunlu">Qara Qoyunlu · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
